GitOps Security Best Practices

✨ Hello DevOps Learners!
Continuing my GitOps learning journey after completing Ansible, today I’m focusing on GitOps Security Best Practices — a crucial topic for building secure and production-ready DevOps systems 🚀
Security in GitOps ensures that only authorized changes are deployed and sensitive data is protected.

🔹 RBAC (Role-Based Access Control)
 RBAC helps in defining who can access and modify resources.
Assign roles like admin, developer, viewer
Restrict permissions based on responsibilities
Prevent unauthorized changes

🔹 Access Control
 Proper access control ensures:
Only approved users can push changes to Git
Deployment actions are controlled
Sensitive operations require authentication
This reduces the risk of accidental or malicious changes.

🔹 Secure Repositories
 Git repositories must be secured by:
Using private repositories
Enabling branch protection rules
Enforcing pull request approvals
Scanning for secrets and vulnerabilities

🔹 Additional Best Practices
Avoid storing secrets in plain text
Use encrypted secrets (Sealed Secrets / Vault)
Enable audit logs for tracking changes
Use least privilege principle

💡 Key Insight:
 Strong security in GitOps ensures that your infrastructure is safe, controlled, and protected from unauthorized access.
